<p>The first estimate on a hail claim often looks complete. A check arrives, a scope is written, and it feels settled. Then the roofer starts work and the numbers stop adding up. Decking is soft under the shingles, vents were left off, and nobody priced the windstorm inspection. That is where <strong>hail damage claim supplements</strong> come in. A supplement is not an argument with the insurer. It is a documented request to bring the estimate in line with what the repair actually requires.</p>

<p>On the Coastal Bend, scopes miss items for predictable reasons: storm volume, quick inspections, and local requirements that generic estimates do not always include. This guide covers what tends to get missed and how to present it.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Claim-Supplements-What-Carriers-Miss-in-Their-Scope-3-1024x683.webp" alt="Three roofing and claims professionals on a shingle roof overlooking a coastal neighborhood and fishing pier" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Coastal roofs carry extra requirements, from windstorm inspections to corrosion-resistant fasteners, that a quick scope may leave out.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>What is a hail damage claim supplement?</h2>

<p>A supplement is a formal revision to an existing claim estimate. It adds what was missed, corrects what was underestimated, and accounts for what was found once repairs began. It is tied to the same date of loss and the same claim number.</p>

<p>Supplements usually become necessary when:</p>
<ul>
<li>Hidden damage appears after tear-off, such as damaged decking or failed underlayment.</li>
<li>Required components were left out of the original scope.</li>
<li>Unit pricing does not match current local labor and material costs.</li>
<li>Permit, code, and inspection items were not included.</li>
<li>Quantities were measured wrong, such as roof squares, ridge length, or waste.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why initial hail scopes fall short</h2>

<p>This is not always about bad faith. After a spring hail event, and Nueces County records show most of its hail reports in April and May, adjusters may be assigned many claims at once. Inspections are short. Estimates are often built from standard templates, sometimes by a desk reviewer who never stood on the roof.</p>

<p>Most insurers and contractors use Xactimate, an estimating program with regional price lists. It is detailed, but it does not think for anyone. If a line item is not entered, it does not exist in the estimate. If the quantity is wrong, so is the price. Small omissions add up across a full roof.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Claim-Supplements-What-Carriers-Miss-in-Their-Scope-5-1024x683.webp" alt="Collage of claims professionals pointing out hail impacts on a coastal roof and a roofer inspecting decking during tear-off" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Some of the most important supplement evidence is gathered during tear-off, when decking and underlayment are exposed.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Commonly missed items in hail claim scopes</h2>

<p>Here are the items that most often go missing, why they matter, and what supports them.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Why it matters</th><th>Supporting evidence</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Starter strip and ridge cap</td><td>Separate materials from field shingles; often omitted or under-measured</td><td>Roof measurements, manufacturer installation instructions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drip edge</td><td>Commonly required at eaves and rakes on shingle roofs under the International Residential Code</td><td>Code citation, photos of existing edge metal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Underlayment and decking</td><td>Damaged or deteriorated decking is often found only after tear-off</td><td>Tear-off photos with measurements, sheet count</td></tr>
<tr><td>Step, counter, and valley flashing; pipe jacks</td><td>Waterproofing details that are often not reusable</td><td>Close-ups of dented or damaged flashing and boots</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vents (box, ridge, turbine, exhaust caps)</td><td>Metal vents dent easily in hail and are frequently left off</td><td>Photos of each vent with scale</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gutters, downspouts, screens, garage doors</td><td>Hail damages more than shingles</td><td>Elevation-by-elevation photos</td></tr>
<tr><td>Detach and reset</td><td>Satellite dishes, solar panels, AC line sets, and roof-mounted equipment must be removed and reinstalled</td><td>Photos and contractor invoice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Steep and high charges</td><td>Steeper and taller roofs take more labor and safety equipment</td><td>Pitch measurement, story count</td></tr>
<tr><td>Debris removal and dumpster</td><td>Tear-off waste must be hauled and disposed of</td><td>Dumpster or haul invoice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interior repairs and paint</td><td>Leaks from hail damage can stain ceilings and wet insulation</td><td>Interior photos, moisture readings</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>For a full documentation process, see our guide on <a href="/document-hail-damage-for-an-insurance-claim/">how to document hail damage for an insurance claim</a>.</p>

<h2>Corpus Christi permit and windstorm items</h2>

<p>This is where local knowledge matters most, and where generic scopes often fall short.</p>

<p><strong>City permit.</strong> The City of Corpus Christi requires a permit to re-roof. According to the city’s residential remodels and repairs guidance, the roofing submission includes a Residential Building Permit Application (Form 1003), a detailed scope of work naming the roofing method and materials, and a completed TDI WPI-1 form from the windstorm engineer or inspector responsible for windstorm compliance. The city’s homeowner permitting guide also lists replacing more than 100 square feet of shingles, changing roof material, and removing and replacing decking among work that needs a permit.</p>

<p><strong>Windstorm inspection.</strong> Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties are in the TDI windstorm catastrophe area. TDI’s Windstorm Inspection Program covers "re-roofs or roof repairs," and inspections are done by appointed Texas-licensed engineers or TDI inspectors. Starting April 1, 2026, WPI-1 applications must be certified to the 2024 IRC or IBC. Structures repaired on or after January 1, 1988 need a certificate of compliance (WPI-8) to stay eligible for TWIA coverage, so skipping this step can affect future insurability.</p>

<p><strong>Code-driven upgrades.</strong> If current code requires something the old roof did not have, such as a specific fastening pattern or underlayment, the extra cost may be covered under ordinance or law coverage, up to its limit. Code requirements alone do not create coverage, so check your policy. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page summarizes what the city has adopted.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Local cost item</th><th>Where it comes from</th><th>How to support it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Re-roof permit fee</td><td>City of Corpus Christi Development Services</td><td>Permit receipt from the city’s online portal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Windstorm engineer or inspector (WPI-1)</td><td>TDI Windstorm Inspection Program</td><td>Engineer invoice, WPI-1 copy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Code-required materials or methods</td><td>Adopted residential code and windstorm requirements</td><td>Code section, engineer notes, ordinance or law limit from the policy</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

