<h2>When the items are right but the numbers are wrong</h2>

<p>Not every supplement is about missing line items. Sometimes the scope lists the right work but undercounts it or prices it low. These are the quantity and pricing issues that come up most often:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Roof measurements.</strong> Squares, ridge and hip length, eave and rake length, and valley length should match a measurement report or field measurements. A few squares short on a large roof adds up.</li>
<li><strong>Waste.</strong> Cut-up roofs with hips, valleys, and dormers need more waste allowance than a simple gable roof. A flat waste figure may not fit the roof.</li>
<li><strong>Pitch and stories.</strong> Steep-slope and high-roof labor charges depend on the actual pitch and building height. Measure them and include photos.</li>
<li><strong>Material type.</strong> Laminated (architectural) shingles, metal panels, and tile carry different prices than basic three-tab shingles. The estimate should match what is actually on the roof.</li>
<li><strong>Price timing.</strong> After a regional storm, material and labor costs can shift. Estimating price lists are updated over time, so ask whether the estimate uses the current local price list.</li>
<li><strong>Coordination of trades.</strong> When a repair needs several trades, such as roofing, gutters, HVAC, painting, and drywall, general contractor overhead and profit may be appropriate. Whether it applies depends on the job and the policy.</li>
</ul>

<p>For each pricing item, show your math. A measurement report, a supplier quote, or a contractor invoice is far more persuasive than a single total with no breakdown.</p>

<h2>How to submit a hail claim supplement</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Compare line by line.</strong> Put the insurer’s estimate next to your contractor’s scope and mark every difference.</li>
<li><strong>Document each missing item.</strong> Photos, measurements, invoices, and code references. Tear-off photos should show the item and a tape measure in the same frame.</li>
<li><strong>Notify the insurer before covering hidden damage.</strong> If rotten decking is found during tear-off, tell the insurer and give it a chance to see it, or at least photograph it thoroughly before it is replaced.</li>
<li><strong>Write a clear request.</strong> Reference the claim number and date of loss, list each added or corrected item with its cost, and attach the support.</li>
<li><strong>Track the response.</strong> Keep a written log. Follow up in writing if you do not hear back.</li>
</ol>

<p>An organized <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> is easier for a desk reviewer to approve than a single total with no breakdown. Our <a href="/claim-calculator/">claim calculator</a> can help you see how deductibles and depreciation affect what you actually receive.</p>

<h2>Depreciation, ACV, and getting the rest of the money</h2>

<p>Under many replacement cost policies, the insurer pays actual cash value first (replacement cost minus depreciation) and releases recoverable depreciation after repairs are done, within a deadline in the policy. When you submit the final invoice, include any supplemented items so depreciation on those items is released too. Missing the policy deadline for completing repairs can forfeit recoverable depreciation, so check that date early.</p>

<h2>TWIA hail claims and supplements</h2>

<p>Many coastal homes have hail coverage through TWIA, not the homeowners carrier. TWIA claims follow Texas Insurance Code Chapter 2210. Once TWIA sends a notice accepting all or part of a claim, you have 60 days to demand appraisal if you disagree with the amount, and TWIA may grant 30 more days if you ask in writing with good cause within 15 days after that period ends. If you expect to supplement, do not let those windows close while waiting on a contractor. Ask TWIA in writing how it will handle additional documentation, and keep copies. See our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page for more.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Claim-Supplements-What-Carriers-Miss-in-Their-Scope-4-1024x683.webp" alt="Roofer removing damaged shingles to expose decking while an adjuster records items on a clipboard" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Record hidden damage during tear-off before it is covered up. Once new decking is installed, the evidence is gone.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>When a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster can help</h2>

<p>Many supplements are handled by the owner and roofer. A public adjuster may be useful when the gap between the insurer’s estimate and the real cost is large, when the insurer will not respond, or when the claim involves both TWIA and a homeowners policy. A public adjuster represents the policyholder, reviews the policy, prepares a line-item estimate, and presents the supplement. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

